# DID SERVERLESS / P2P IDENTITY+DATA SYSTEMS SUSTAIN? — MEASURED STATUS, 2026-09-04

## 1. IROH / n0 (number zero, "n0, inc.") — THE CENTRAL CASE

**Iroh is a no-server system that runs servers, and as of 2026 sells them.**

**n0 operates the default relays, by their own documentation.** https://docs.iroh.computer/concepts/relays (the old `iroh.computer/docs/concepts/relay` 308-redirects here): *"Public relays are operated by n0.computer and hardcoded into Iroh, available free of charge"* — with *"no uptime or performance guarantees"* and *"rate-limit traffic to prevent abuse."* Regions: US, EU, Singapore.

**Commercialization is live and priced.** https://www.iroh.computer/pricing (fetched 2026-09-04):
| Tier | Price | Included |
|---|---|---|
| Community | $0 | public relays US/EU/SG, 7-day metrics, rate-limited, no uptime guarantee |
| Pro | **$19/mo** | 10,000 concurrent connections, 5 MB/s rate limit, **100 GB egress**, authenticated access, 30-day metrics |
| Dedicated | **$199/mo per region** (add-on to Pro) | 60,000 concurrent connections, custom regions/DNS, unlimited rate, **250 GB egress**, version locking |
| Enterprise | custom | BYOC / on-prem / multi-cloud, SLAs, dedicated support engineer |

Overages: **$0.003 per endpoint**, **$0.09/GB egress**, **$1.49 per 1,000 metric data-points/minute** above 10K DPM. Signup at `services.iroh.computer`. Homepage (https://www.iroh.computer/) sells "Hosting / Observability / Enterprise" and names commercial users: Datum, Nous Research, Mesh LLM.

**Scale numbers, from n0 itself:**
- **"The public relays we run have seen more than 200 million endpoints created, in the last 30 days alone."** — https://www.iroh.computer/blog/v1, "Iroh 1.0 — Dial Keys, not IPs", **June 15, 2026**.
- Earlier: **"half a million unique nodes hitting the public network in a thirty day span,"** and *"that number roughly doubles when we talk to our partners running their own iroh relays"*; **"at least 40 projects building on iroh."** — https://www.iroh.computer/blog/road-to-1-0, **Oct 28, 2024**.

**Hole-punching / direct-connection success rates — PARTIALLY REACHED.** No dedicated measurement post exists on the current blog. `https://www.iroh.computer/blog/iroh-perf` and `/blog/how-well-does-hole-punching-work` both return **HTTP 404**. I enumerated all 82 blog slugs from the index; there is no such post. What n0 does publish:
- **"Roughly 90% of network conditions allow direct P2P connections"** — https://docs.iroh.computer/concepts/relays (no methodology, no N, no date).
- **"It's normal to see 95% of data transferred in a connection pass directly between devices."** — v1 post, 2026-06-15. This is a *bytes* figure, not a *connection-success* figure.
- The 2023 Number Zero Notion page "Iroh (NAT) Hole Punching" (`number-zero.notion.site/Iroh-Hole-Punching-b9a38cc228fb47328a73e71d10520418`, HN 2023-07-16) is a JS-only Notion shell — **NOT REACHED**.
- The retrospective https://www.iroh.computer/blog/the-road-to-iroh-1-0 (2026-07-09, Rüdiger Klaehn) describes hole punching qualitatively only: *"once we merged it we had hole punch rates to be proud of… in the few corporate networks where hole punching was impossible, at least we could still transfer data via relays."* No percentages. It also states outright: *"Our goal was to get holepunching to work reliably, **even at the expense of compromising p2p purity**. If we would need a small server to help, so be it."*

**The relays are a real, failing, load-bearing dependency.** https://www.iroh.computer/blog/relay-down-a-post-mortem — outage **Nov 5, 2024**: degraded 11:00 GMT, **full outage by 17:00 GMT**, resolved 23:01 GMT; memory leaks under connection load, logs filled disk. **~1 million new nodes onboarded during the incident; peak 100,000 concurrent connections before failure.** Detected internally only at 22:50 GMT — ~12 hours late. n0 runs multi-region `iroh-relay` + `iroh-dns-server`; an underpowered Asia node was migrated to bigger hardware.

**Relay abuse forced authentication.** https://www.iroh.computer/blog/authenticated-relays (July 30, 2026): relay URLs are *"visible to anyone watching a connection get established"* and shipped in client bundles; *"Someone finds your relay URL in a public repo, a client bundle, or a screenshot, and starts spamming your infrastructure until it falls over."* Signed capability tokens tied to **API keys**; **default-on for relays deployed from June 2026 onward.** This is the moment the "serverless" network acquired an account system.

**Public relay support has an end date.** From the v1 post: public relay operation for **v0.35x ends Dec 31, 2026**; for **v0.9x and 1.0.0-rcX, Sept 30, 2026**. *"Public relays are rate-limited for relayed traffic, which can change at any time."*

**Engineering health: strong.** github.com/n0-computer/iroh — **12,450 stars, 749 forks, 162 open issues, last push 2026-09-04**. Releases: v1.0.0 2026-06-15, v1.0.1 06-29, v1.0.2 07-06 (relay security fix), v1.0.3 07-20, v1.1.0 08-25 (security fixes 09-01). Multi-person team commits daily. HN: Iroh 1.0 hit **1,398 points** (2026-06-15). Real downstream apps exist: LinXiv, Lexidraw, Appa, Pigeons (SSH), git-remote-iroh, go-iroh, alt-sendme, iroh-drop.

**n0 funding — NOT REACHED.** n0.computer describes itself as *"an open R&D organization focused on efficient distributed systems"* with *"70+ years combined experience"*, revenue from products + "Services & Support" + open source. **No funding amount, round, or investor is disclosed on n0.computer, iroh.computer, the GitHub org (`number zero`, created 2022-03-05, 122 public repos), or any HN thread I searched.** Whether venture-backed is unresolved from primary sources. The entity is a for-profit: pages footer **"© 2025 n0, inc."**

**Origin note (relevant to the pattern):** iroh started as a Rust IPFS implementation and abandoned it. https://www.iroh.computer/blog/a-new-direction-for-iroh (Feb 17, 2023) gives the measured reason: *"the current interop-focused Iroh needs at least 2,000 simultaneous p2p connections to serve as a gateway; sends on the order of 1,000 messages to retrieve a single 256KB block of data; takes on the order of seconds to resolve DHT queries; and **cannot reliably remain connected to the vast majority of peers on the network, if connection is possible at all**. We need those numbers to be on the order of 200 peers; less than 1 message per block; and 95%+ reliability."* The IPFS implementation was renamed **Beetle** and put in maintenance mode.

