## 8. IPFS / PROTOCOL LABS — LAYOFFS, GOVERNANCE OFFLOAD, GATEWAY EXODUS

**Layoffs, measured.** https://pl.xyz/blog/2023-02-03-crypto-winter-update/ (was protocol.ai, 308-redirects to pl.xyz), **Feb 3, 2023**, Juan Benet, "Focusing our strategy to weather crypto winter": **89 roles eliminated, ~21% of the workforce.** Divisions hit: PL Corp, PL Member Services, **Network Goods**, PL Outercore, PL Starfleet. Stated cause: *"High inflation leading to high interest rates, low investment, and tougher markets have rocked companies and industries globally"* — and a need to *"focus our headcount against the most impactful and business critical efforts."* Pre/post headcount not disclosed. (HN, 124 pts, 2023-02-04.) **No second round of layoffs was found in the pl.xyz blog index — a 2024 round is NOT REACHED / not evidenced.**

**Governance offload, Nov 14, 2023** — pl.xyz blog, "Advancing IPFS and libp2p Governance": *"IPFS and libp2p are officially taking big steps forward in project maturity, with independent foundations and funding structures."* In plain terms, PL stopped being the funder of record.

**The core team left, April 2024.** Per Cloudflare's own post (below): Interplanetary Shipyard is *"an engineering collective… consist[ing] of core IPFS developers and maintainers who transitioned from Protocol Labs in April 2024"*, now maintaining Kubo, Helia, and go-libp2p as an independent entity.

**Protocol Labs today is not an IPFS company.** The pl.xyz blog index (2023–2026) is dominated by "innovation network" and R&D-pipeline framing — Gensyn, Spice AI, ZK, **neurotech, robotics, satellites**, stablecoins and cross-border payments. Last strategy post: "Driving Breakthroughs: The Impact of Our Innovation Network," Jan 29, 2025.

**Gateway centralization — and its collapse.** https://blog.cloudflare.com/cloudflares-public-ipfs-gateways-and-supporting-interplanetary-shipyard (2024-05-16, HN):
- Cloudflare **shut down its public IPFS gateway**. Transition began **May 14, 2024**; **after August 14, 2024 `cloudflare-ipfs.com` and `cf-ipfs.com` no longer connect to IPFS**; traffic redirected to **ipfs.io / dweb.link** in the interim.
- The traffic did not decentralize — it was **handed to the other centralized gateway operator.** `ipfs.io` now 301-redirects to `about.ipfs.io`.
- **Gateway traffic share numbers: NOT REACHED.** No operator publishes them. Cloudflare's post gives no volumes; Shipyard's own https://blog.ipfs.tech/2024-shipyard-improving-ipfs-on-the-web/ (2024-11-28) gives **no request counts, no cost figures, no funding amounts** — only the admission that *"public gateways became entrenched as the default to access IPFS content on the web to the detriment of the principles of IPFS"*, plus a donate link.

**Gateway operators are also a legal liability sink:** *"Publishers carpet-bomb IPFS gateway operators with DMCA notices"* (torrentfreak, 2023-06-25, HN 234 pts); Cloudflare blocked pirated content on its gateway 2023-03-25 (HN 304 pts); "Copyright as Harassment: The DMCA Attack on IPFS Gateways" (Techdirt, 2023-07-07). This is a running cost that falls on whoever runs the server the network claims not to need.

**libp2p circuit-relay economics: NOT REACHED.** No published cost model, relay-operator census, or reimbursement scheme was findable. No HN discussion exists. This is a genuine negative result: **the economics of who pays for libp2p relays appear to be undocumented.**

**Independent measurement:** the last substantive academic IPFS workload measurement I could locate is *"Studying the workload of a fully decentralized Web3 system: IPFS"* (arXiv:2212.07375, 2022-12-14). More recent IPFS arXiv work is about abuse, not health: *"Hello, won't you tell me your name?: Investigating Anonymity Abuse in IPFS"* (arXiv:2506.04307, 2025-06-04). (OpenAlex was budget-exhausted this session — broader lit sweep NOT REACHED.)

---

## 9. BLUESKY / ATPROTO — EXPLICITLY NOT SERVERLESS, AND THE COST IS PUBLISHED

**This is the only system in this report whose infrastructure costs are measured, published, and cheap.**

**Bryan Newbold, "A Full-Network Relay for $34 a Month"** — https://whtwnd.com/bnewbold.net/3lo7a2a4qxg2l , **2025-08-27** (HN 2025-05-02):
- **$34/month all-in** ($30/mo base VPS + tax).
- **8 vCPU, 16 GB RAM, 160 GB disk, 1 Gbps unmetered.**
- Storage in use: **21 GB relay data, 2.4 GB PostgreSQL, 114 MB logs** — because it runs a **2-hour** backfill window (default 72 h) and does **not** archive full repos.
- **30 Mbps sustained** for the outbound firehose WebSocket.
- **600 events/sec** (April 2025 peak); 2,000 events/sec historical sustained.
- CPU ~5–15% user, ~1–3% sys; Postgres **766 tps**, 95.18% cache hit, 1.73 GB DB; RAM 2.06 GB (13.48%).

**Bryan Newbold, "Notes on Running a Full-Network atproto Relay (July 2024)"** — https://whtwnd.com/bnewbold.net/3kwzl7tye6u2y , published 2025-08-27, content dated July 2024. The *archiving* relay, i.e. the expensive one:
- **$152/month + $92 one-time setup** (OVH bare metal; a comparable later option $153/mo + $93).
- **12 vCPU (Xeon E-2136 / EPYC 4244P), 32 GB ECC, 2×1.92 TB NVMe (3.84 TB), 1 Gbit/s unmetered.**
- After full backfill: **447 GB PostgreSQL + 722 GB CAR storage ≈ 1.2 TB of 3.84 TB used.**
- Steady state: 3–6 MB/s read, 7–14 MB/s write, **514 tps**, 172–313 KB/s network.
- Headroom: could absorb *"an order of magnitude more event rate"* before disk becomes the binding constraint within ~1 year.

**The ceiling figure**, from "Reply on Bluesky and Decentralization," https://whtwnd.com/bnewbold.net/3lbvbtqrg5t2t , **2024-11-27**: a full-network, **fully archiving** relay needs *"on the order of **16 TBytes of fast NVMe disk**."*

So the honest range: **$34/mo (non-archiving, 2-hour window) → ~$152/mo (archiving, 1.2 TB) → 16 TB NVMe (full archive).**

**Firehose volume, Jaz (Jaz Volpert), "Jetstream: Shrinking the AT Proto Firehose by >99%"** — https://jazco.dev/2024/09/24/jetstream/ , **2024-09-24**:
- Full firehose: **~24 GB/day pre-surge → 232 GB/day** during the Brazil X-ban surge (**~1,300% activity increase**). **≈3.16 TB/month** for the full protocol firehose.
- Jetstream (JSON, uncompressed): **~41 GB/day ≈ 400 GB/month.**
- Jetstream + zstd dictionary: **~18 GB/day peak**; **~850 MB/day** for posts only ≈ **25.5 GB/month**.
- Compression: **482 bytes → 211 bytes/event, ratio 0.44.** Dictionary trained on 100,000 real events.
- Memory **<16 MB resident**; <8 GB buffer for a 24-hour playback window; **200k events/sec** dropping to 28k/sec per consumer in streaming mode with 12 concurrent consumers.
- **Test infrastructure: a $5/month OVH VPS.**

**The company, by contrast, is heavily capitalized and centralized.** https://bsky.social/about/blog/03-19-2026-series-b , posted **March 19, 2026**, reporting an **April 2025** round:
- **$100M Series B led by Bain Capital Crypto**, with Alumni Ventures, Anthos Capital, Bloomberg Beta, Knight Foundation, True Ventures. Round led internally by founder Jay Graber.
- **13 million users (Series A, Oct 2024) → over 43 million** global users.
- Ecosystem: **>1,000 apps** used weekly on atproto; **>400,000 SDK downloads/month**; **~20 billion public records** in the Atmosphere.
- Bluesky is a Public Benefit Corporation.

**Governance churn:** Jay Graber stepped down as CEO to Chief Innovation Officer **2026-03-09**; Toni Schneider interim, made **permanent CEO 2026-07-10**. Also: **DDoS-driven service interruptions, April 16–20, 2026** (four consecutive blog posts).

**The pattern this proves:** a system that admits it has servers publishes exact server costs, and those costs are two orders of magnitude below what the "serverless" projects imply they avoid. Independent full-network participation costs **$34–$152/month**; the operator raised **$100M** to run the *centralized* parts (app view, moderation, hosting 43M users' PDSes).

---

## CROSS-CUTTING MEASURED FINDINGS

**1. Every "no server" system in this set ships or depends on servers, and the server is the thing that fails.** Iroh: n0-operated hardcoded relays, one 12-hour outage at 100k concurrent connections (2024-11-05), abuse forcing API-key authentication (June 2026), and an announced **end-of-life date for free public relay operation (Dec 31, 2026)**. SSB: pubs, whose server software has been dead since 2022-06-17. IPFS: public gateways, of which the largest operator (Cloudflare) exited in Aug 2024. Ceramic: a *centralized* anchor service (CAS), by their own word, now scheduled for shutdown.

**2. The relay/gateway is where a P2P project becomes a business or dies.** Iroh took the business path and now has a $19/$199/mo price sheet and API keys. Cloudflare took the exit path. Briar and SSB had no path.

**3. Grant funding produced finished specifications and abandoned implementations.** Willow (NLnet/NGI, grant 957073) has a complete, candidate-status spec set — and an **archived** reference Rust implementation, a dormant JS implementation, a dependabot-only iroh integration, and **no shipped application**. Earthstar has been in v11 beta since 2024-05-21. Briar's own post names the mechanism exactly: no funding → spare-time work → decision to shut down.

**4. Three of eight are effectively over.** Ceramic (deprecated by its operator; blog silent 17 months; forum offline; pivoted to an AI token). SSB (pub software 4 years dead; flagship funded client migrated to Nostr). Briar (decided to shut down in 2025, currently on security-only life support). Two more are one-person efforts: Veilid (Rioux, 9 of last 10 commits, still 0.5.x after 3 years) and OrbitDB (Hayden Young, 38 commits/year, donation-funded).

**5. Only two sustained, and both have a company and a revenue model.** Iroh (n0, inc. — daily commits, 1.0 after 4 years and 65 versions, ~200M endpoints/30d on **its own relays**, priced services). Bluesky ($100M Series B, 43M users, a PBC) — which never claimed to be serverless and publishes its costs.

**6. Unresolved / NOT REACHED:** n0's funding structure and investors; a dedicated n0 hole-punching success-rate publication (the two URLs specified return 404 and no such post exists among the 82 blog slugs — the only published figures are the undocumented "roughly 90%" in docs and "95% of data" in the v1 post); Planetary's exact shutdown date and dated statement; IPFS public-gateway traffic share; libp2p circuit-relay economics (apparently undocumented anywhere); Veilid and OrbitDB funding totals; NLnet grant amounts for Willow.
